# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Zephaniah'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Day of the LORD, the Meek Remnant, and the King in Their Midst'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Zephaniah is “the word of the LORD which came unto Zephaniah… in the days of Josiah the son of Amon, king of Judah” (Zeph. 1:1). It speaks of the great day of the LORD, calls the meek to seek the LORD, promises a pure language and an afflicted and poor people who trust in His name, and closes with the song of the daughter of Zion (Zeph. 1:14; 2:3; 3:9, 12, 14–17).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to Judah and Jerusalem about the day of the LORD, the remnant of Israel and the king of Israel in their midst (Zeph. 1:1; 3:13–15).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Zephaniah', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Great Day of the LORD (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The LORD prepares a sacrifice, and the great day is near (Zeph. 1:1, 7, 14).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Seek the LORD (Ch. 2)'), ('p', 'The meek of the earth are called to seek righteousness and meekness (Zeph. 2:1, 3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Nations Gathered, a Remnant Left (Ch. 3:1–13)'), ('p', 'The LORD gathers the nations, gives a pure language, and leaves an afflicted and poor people (Zeph. 3:8, 9, 12–13).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Song of Zion (Ch. 3:14–20)'), ('p', 'The king of Israel is in the midst of Zion, and the captivity is turned back (Zeph. 3:14–15, 17, 20).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Zephaniah', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Day at Hand</b> — “The day of the LORD is at hand” (Zeph. 1:7).', '<b>Seek the LORD</b> — “Seek ye the LORD, all ye meek of the earth… it may be ye shall be hid in the day of the LORD’s anger” (Zeph. 2:3).', '<b>Nations Gathered</b> — “My determination is to gather the nations, that I may assemble the kingdoms” (Zeph. 3:8).', '<b>A Pure Language</b> — “Then will I turn to the people a pure language, that they may all call upon the name of the LORD” (Zeph. 3:9).', '<b>The Remnant of Israel</b> — “The remnant of Israel shall not do iniquity, nor speak lies” (Zeph. 3:13).', '<b>The King in Their Midst</b> — “The king of Israel, even the LORD, is in the midst of thee” (Zeph. 3:15).', '<b>Captivity Turned Back</b> — “I turn back your captivity before your eyes, saith the LORD” (Zeph. 3:20).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Zephaniah', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Zephaniah points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The King of Israel</b> — The LORD is “the king of Israel” in the midst of the daughter of Zion (Zeph. 3:15).', '<b>He Will Save</b> — “He will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy” (Zeph. 3:17).', '<b>The Day of the LORD</b> — The great day of the LORD is described in Zeph. 1:14 and read beside Joel 1:15 and Obadiah 1:15 as written.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Zephaniah', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Zephaniah speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Zephaniah operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Zephaniah in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Zephaniah reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Judah and Jerusalem addressed in the days of Josiah (Zeph. 1:1)', 'the remnant of Israel and the daughter of Zion (Zeph. 3:13–14)', 'the king of Israel in the midst of Zion (Zeph. 3:15)', 'the captivity turned back (Zeph. 3:20)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Zephaniah that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Zephaniah contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Day at Hand — “the day of the LORD is at hand.” (Zeph. 1:7).', 'The Great Day Near — “The great day of the LORD is near, it is near, and hasteth greatly.” (Zeph. 1:14).', 'Nations Gathered — “my determination is to gather the nations, that I may assemble the kingdoms.” (Zeph. 3:8).', 'A Pure Language — “I turn to the people a pure language.” (Zeph. 3:9).', 'The Remnant of Israel — “The remnant of Israel shall not do iniquity, nor speak lies.” (Zeph. 3:13).', 'The King in the Midst — “the king of Israel, even the LORD, is in the midst of thee.” (Zeph. 3:15).', 'Captivity Turned Back — “when I turn back your captivity before your eyes, saith the LORD.” (Zeph. 3:20).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Zephaniah Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Zephaniah and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Zephaniah teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Zephaniah', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Day at Hand', '“the day of the LORD is at hand.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 1:7'], ['The Great Day Near', '“The great day of the LORD is near, it is near, and hasteth greatly.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 1:14'], ['Nations Gathered', '“my determination is to gather the nations, that I may assemble the kingdoms.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 3:8'], ['A Pure Language', '“I turn to the people a pure language.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 3:9'], ['The Remnant of Israel', '“The remnant of Israel shall not do iniquity, nor speak lies.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 3:13'], ['The King in the Midst', '“the king of Israel, even the LORD, is in the midst of thee.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 3:15'], ['Captivity Turned Back', '“when I turn back your captivity before your eyes, saith the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Zeph. 3:20']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Zephaniah that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Zephaniah teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Seek the LORD', '“Seek ye the LORD, all ye meek of the earth.”', 'Zeph. 2:3'], ['Trust in His Name', '“they shall trust in the name of the LORD.”', 'Zeph. 3:12'], ['Mighty to Save', '“The LORD thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save.”', 'Zeph. 3:17'], ['Rejoicing in Love', '“he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing.”', 'Zeph. 3:17']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Zephaniah without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Zephaniah in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Seek the LORD</b> — Seek righteousness and meekness (Zeph. 2:3).', '<b>Trust in His name</b> — An afflicted and poor people may trust (Zeph. 3:12).', '<b>Rest on His strength</b> — He is mighty and will save (Zeph. 3:17).', '<b>Know His delight</b> — He will rest in His love (Zeph. 3:17).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Zephaniah', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Zephaniah in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Great Day of the LORD (Ch. 1) — The day at hand. <i>(Zeph. 1:1, 7, 14)</i>', 'Seek the LORD (Ch. 2) — The meek hid in the day. <i>(Zeph. 2:3)</i>', 'Nations Gathered, a Remnant Left (Ch. 3:1–13) — A pure language. <i>(Zeph. 3:8–9, 12–13)</i>', 'The Song of Zion (Ch. 3:14–20) — The king in their midst. <i>(Zeph. 3:14–15, 17, 20)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Zephaniah, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward seeking the LORD, humility, and rejoicing in His salvation without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Zephaniah simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Zephaniah stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
